1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,000
I recently interviewed Robert Caro.
He's spent the last 50 years writing  

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,120
a 4-volume biography of Lyndon Johnson, 
and he spent another 7 years before that  

3
00:00:09,119 --> 00:00:13,359
writing the biography of Robert Moses, 
who was the master builder of New York. 

4
00:00:13,359 --> 00:00:17,839
His books are widely regarded as the best 
explorations of political power that have  

5
00:00:17,839 --> 00:00:22,320
ever been written, and they also happen 
to be literally my favorite books ever. 

6
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:26,240
Whenever I meet somebody new, one of the 
first questions that they often ask me is,  

7
00:00:26,239 --> 00:00:29,599
"Who's the person you most want to 
interview that you haven't yet?" 

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,160
And for 6 years, I've answered that 
question in the same way: Robert Caro. 

9
00:00:34,159 --> 00:00:37,119
I've spent a lot of time trying 
to make this interview happen. 

10
00:00:37,119 --> 00:00:42,159
I've chased down his agent, I've called 
his publisher, I've contacted the  

11
00:00:42,159 --> 00:00:46,319
museums which are hosting his archives.
I've even sent him handwritten letters. 

12
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:49,600
I finally got to sit down with him, and 
I'm very happy with how it turned out. 

13
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,240
It'll be out tomorrow. In the meantime, as I was 
preparing for this interview and rereading all  

14
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:59,840
of these biographies, I was writing down all the 
lessons that they laid out about how to accumulate  

15
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:13,200
and use power, and this video essay is the result.
Robert Moses was the master builder of New York. 

16
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:18,400
Our distinguished guest was Mr. Robert 
Moses, the nation's foremost city planner. 

17
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,320
He built more of the physical 
city than anybody before or since. 

18
00:01:22,319 --> 00:01:28,000
He built the Triborough and Verrazzano bridges, 
the Cross Bronx Expressway, Jones Beach, Lincoln  

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,799
Center, Shea Stadium, 2 giant power dams upstate, 
in addition to hundreds of parks and pools. 

20
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:43,599
Of the 899 miles of highways completed in New 
York State by 1964, Moses had built 672 of them. 

21
00:01:43,599 --> 00:01:47,599
His projects collectively cost hundreds 
of billions of dollars in today's money. 

22
00:01:47,599 --> 00:01:53,280
For more than 30 years, Robert Moses was the 
most powerful person in New York, more powerful  

23
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:58,000
than any elected mayor or governor, despite 
the fact that he was not elected to anything. 

24
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,879
As I've heard it, ad nauseam, for the last 30-odd 
years, they say, "You don't have to be elected." 

25
00:02:04,879 --> 00:02:08,079
My stock reply to them is, 
"You don't have to be, either." 

26
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:10,719
How did he get that much 
power, and how did he keep it? 

27
00:02:10,719 --> 00:02:14,560
Part of the answer is that Moses 
wrote the laws that governed him. 

28
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:18,400
He was called the best bill drafter in 
Albany, and he used that skill to write  

29
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,360
himself powers that the legislators voting 
on his bills didn't know they were granting. 

30
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:29,760
The political genius of Robert Moses is perhaps 
best illustrated by his use of public authorities. 

31
00:02:29,759 --> 00:02:33,359
These are government-created corporations 
that build city infrastructure. 

32
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:38,720
Public authorities sell bonds to pay for 
a specific project, say, a new bridge. 

33
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:43,280
Once the bridge is built, the authority would 
charge drivers a toll until it had collected  

34
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:47,120
enough money to pay off its bonds.
Then the authority would tear down  

35
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,120
the toll booths, hand the bridge over 
to the city, and go out of existence. 

36
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:54,159
At least, that was the idea.
That's what Albany assumed would  

37
00:02:54,159 --> 00:03:00,079
happen when the New York State Congress approved 
the Triborough Bridge Authority Act of 1933,  

38
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:06,160
whose final version included, buried within pages 
and pages of legalese, a few seemingly minor  

39
00:03:06,159 --> 00:03:12,000
alterations that were drafted by Robert Moses.
Section 1 of this act clearly stated that  

40
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,360
the authority would dissolve 
once the bonds were paid off. 

41
00:03:15,360 --> 00:03:19,680
Since the bonds could run at most 40 years, and 
the revenues from the bridge would pay back the  

42
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:25,439
initial cost far sooner, it seemed like the 
authority had a clear built-in expiry date. 

43
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:30,560
However, in a subsection of Section 9, Moses 
added a single sentence that said that the  

44
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:36,400
authority could, "from time to time," pay 
off its old bonds by issuing new ones. 

45
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:40,800
And those new ones could be "for 
any other corporate purpose." 

46
00:03:41,919 --> 00:03:46,079
So just before the original bonds were 
about to expire, the authority now had  

47
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:50,880
the right to issue a totally fresh 40-year 
note for a potentially totally different use. 

48
00:03:50,879 --> 00:03:53,439
And what could the authority 
build with all this money? 

49
00:03:53,439 --> 00:03:57,359
The original act let it build 
2 bridges and their approaches. 

50
00:03:57,360 --> 00:04:03,280
Triborough's chairman, Robert Moses, called 
this project more than a mere bridge. "It  

51
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:07,919
will be," he said, "an immense artery of 
travel in the very heart of the city." 

52
00:04:07,919 --> 00:04:11,199
From the island, through the toll 
gate, rides the first vehicle. 

53
00:04:11,199 --> 00:04:15,119
Young men in rented tuxedos, the passengers.
They parked here all week for  

54
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:19,600
the honor of being first to cross.
Moses' amendments also gave this authority the  

55
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:25,439
right to build connecting roads and "facilities 
not inconsistent with the use of the project." 

56
00:04:26,399 --> 00:04:33,039
These rights, when interpreted as creatively 
as Moses intended, would allow Moses to alter  

57
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:39,040
most of the roads, parks, and housing in New York.
For if a road connected to a road which connected  

58
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:43,920
to a road which connected to the bridge, or 
if a park lay along the road that connected  

59
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:48,240
to the road that connected to the road that 
connected to the bridge, surely the Triborough  

60
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:54,240
Authority was entitled to improve and change it.
All this construction could be financed by the  

61
00:04:54,240 --> 00:05:00,000
bridges' enormous toll revenues and the right of 
the Triborough Authority to borrow against them. 

62
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,680
To give some perspective, in 1946, the entire 
New York City government could only borrow  

63
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:11,280
$150 million before hitting its legal debt limit.
That very same year, Triborough  

64
00:05:11,279 --> 00:05:15,599
could borrow $360 million.
So Moses' bridge authority  

65
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:19,760
had access to more than double the amount 
of money as the largest city in America. 

66
00:05:19,759 --> 00:05:24,000
And what if a future governor or mayor 
eventually learned about all this power  

67
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,279
and revenue that had been delegated to this 
authority, and they decided to claw it back? 

68
00:05:29,279 --> 00:05:34,479
Moses had planned for this, too, and 
his solution was so clever that no  

69
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:40,160
lawyer or lawmaker in Albany saw it coming.
The United States Constitution says that no  

70
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:47,920
state shall make "any law impairing the obligation 
of contracts" entered into by a government entity. 

71
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:52,160
A bond issued by a public 
authority is such a contract. 

72
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:59,040
Moses snuck a clause into the act which 
allowed him to add "any other matters  

73
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:03,840
of like or different character, which in 
any way affect the security or protection  

74
00:06:03,839 --> 00:06:10,319
of the bonds" into these bond contracts.
These matters included that Triborough could  

75
00:06:10,319 --> 00:06:13,680
charge whatever tolls it wanted 
and for however long it wanted,  

76
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:19,280
and it could keep the surplus thus generated.
That is to say, Moses could now entrench his  

77
00:06:19,279 --> 00:06:23,119
powers simply by writing them 
into the bonds that he issued. 

78
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:27,920
From the moment those bonds were 
sold, those powers could no longer  

79
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:33,120
be revoked by the legislature or by the 
governor or by the mayor, by anything,  

80
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:38,959
in fact, short of a constitutional convention.
The powers that Moses now had were guaranteed  

81
00:06:38,959 --> 00:06:45,279
for the bonds' entire lifetime, a lifetime which 
could always be extended simply by refinancing. 

82
00:06:45,279 --> 00:06:51,519
So with just a few minor tweaks, Moses had turned 
an act meant to allow for the construction of  

83
00:06:51,519 --> 00:06:57,839
2 bridges into the basis of an institution that 
could build basically anything it wanted anywhere  

84
00:06:57,839 --> 00:07:03,439
in New York, and whose authority to do so was 
protected by the United States Constitution. 

85
00:07:03,439 --> 00:07:08,000
These bond covenants had turned the 
Triborough Authority's powers into granite,  

86
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,879
but nothing in them guaranteed that 
Robert Moses would remain its chairman. 

87
00:07:12,879 --> 00:07:16,959
So how did Moses retain 
this position for 30 years? 

88
00:07:16,959 --> 00:07:21,519
Part of the answer is, again, the law.
Moses drafted the Triborough Act so that  

89
00:07:21,519 --> 00:07:27,279
its commissioners could be removed midterm only 
on "stated charges after a formal hearing." 

90
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:32,319
But any differences he had with the mayor or 
governor were more about what he decided to  

91
00:07:32,319 --> 00:07:36,079
build and whom he displaced to build 
it rather than his personal conduct. 

92
00:07:36,079 --> 00:07:40,719
As Caro himself writes, "Robert 
Moses was not himself corrupt. 

93
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:44,080
He was, in fact, as uninterested 
in obtaining payoffs for himself  

94
00:07:44,079 --> 00:07:49,359
as any public servant who ever lived.
In the politicians' phrase, he was money honest." 

95
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:54,000
So there were never any formal charges to bring.
But this only explains why he wasn't  

96
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,560
fired in the middle of his term.
Why was he reappointed every 6 years? 

97
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:04,480
At his peak, Moses was a hydra with a dozen heads.
He held 12 different government posts at the  

98
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:08,560
same time: city construction coordinator, 
chairman of the State Council of Parks,  

99
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:13,040
chairman of the State Power Authority, and so on.
Some of these positions were appointed by the  

100
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:15,840
mayor, some of them by the 
governor, and so no single  

101
00:08:15,839 --> 00:08:20,399
person could cut off the heads simultaneously.
And removing him from any one position meant that  

102
00:08:20,399 --> 00:08:26,479
you were making an enemy of the man who would 
still be the most powerful person in New York. 

103
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:30,879
More importantly, no mayor or 
governor wanted to fire him. 

104
00:08:30,879 --> 00:08:37,279
The way we remember Robert Moses today is based 
on a reputation that was calcified in the 1970s,  

105
00:08:37,279 --> 00:08:42,365
when The Power Broker came out and when New 
York was experiencing a dark age of crime,  

106
00:08:42,365 --> 00:08:46,639
deindustrialization, and white flight.
Moses got much of the blame for this. 

107
00:08:46,639 --> 00:08:51,919
After all, the subtitle of The Power Broker 
is Robert Moses and the Fall of New York. 

108
00:08:51,919 --> 00:08:57,839
But in my opinion, this crisis had more 
to do with larger structural factors that  

109
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:01,519
had nothing to do with Moses himself.
Cities that he had nothing to do with,  

110
00:09:01,519 --> 00:09:05,679
like Detroit and St. Louis, experienced 
a much more dramatic collapse. 

111
00:09:05,679 --> 00:09:10,000
For the vast majority of his career, 
Moses was incredibly popular. 

112
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,759
In the 1930s and 1940s, New York high schools 
voted Moses the most admired New Yorker. 

113
00:09:15,759 --> 00:09:21,840
This was because he had delivered, over and over 
again, public works which millions of New Yorkers  

114
00:09:21,840 --> 00:09:28,320
got to use and enjoy: the beaches, parks, pools.
They used to dive off a dock into the murky river,  

115
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:31,360
but now it's splashing deluxe 
in a grand up-to-date lido. 

116
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,840
It cost a cool million, and 
the kids say it's worth it. 

117
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:38,800
And he did so at a speed and a 
scale that nobody had seen before. 

118
00:09:38,799 --> 00:09:43,759
In his first 4 months as the city's park 
commissioner, he kept crews working round the  

119
00:09:43,759 --> 00:09:50,319
clock and finished 1,700 park renovations, and he 
did this all without a whiff of personal scandal. 

120
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:55,440
As Caro writes in The Power Broker, "It 
was not unusual at park and playground  

121
00:09:55,440 --> 00:10:02,378
opening ceremonies for children prodded 
by their parents to break into the cheer." 

122
00:10:02,378 --> 00:10:07,759
2, 4, 6, 8. Who do we appreciate? 
Mr. Moses. Mr. Moses. Mr. Moses. 

123
00:10:07,759 --> 00:10:12,080
Importantly, Moses could deliver these 
enormous and popular public works within  

124
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:16,960
a timeframe that fit into a single election cycle.
If the mayor gave Robert Moses the power to build  

125
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:21,200
something, he could expect to be standing at its 
ribbon cutting before he was up for re-election. 

126
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:25,520
Robert Moses, Mayor Wagner, and Cardinal 
Spellman join other dignitaries in a ribbon  

127
00:10:25,519 --> 00:10:29,360
cutting at the Brooklyn side of the bridge.
Today, it's almost unimaginable that the  

128
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:31,919
government could construct 
big things in a year or 2. 

129
00:10:31,919 --> 00:10:37,279
For example, construction on the 2nd 
Avenue Subway in New York began in 1972,  

130
00:10:37,279 --> 00:10:43,120
and the first section was opened in 2017.
Just these initial 2 miles have cost the  

131
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,799
taxpayers $4.5 billion.
A lot of the funding for  

132
00:10:46,799 --> 00:10:50,559
public works comes from Washington.
Cities that receive the most money  

133
00:10:50,559 --> 00:10:54,079
are the ones that have finalized their 
blueprints and can start breaking ground  

134
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:59,360
as soon as a new federal initiative is announced.
Moses had the largest and best team of planners  

135
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:04,639
and engineers in the entire country.
Because of that, by 1936, New York was  

136
00:11:04,639 --> 00:11:08,639
getting 1/7 of the money that the federal 
government had set aside for Depression-era  

137
00:11:08,639 --> 00:11:12,559
work relief for the entire nation.
And the projects that this funding was  

138
00:11:12,559 --> 00:11:17,679
going towards were the ones chosen by Moses.
If the mayor had some different idea about  

139
00:11:17,679 --> 00:11:21,519
what should be constructed, he'd have to 
draw up his own plans, which he didn't  

140
00:11:21,519 --> 00:11:25,919
have the independent technical expertise to do.
So he could either build the projects that Moses  

141
00:11:25,919 --> 00:11:30,159
wanted to build or he could build nothing at all.
Between his ability to get things done,  

142
00:11:30,159 --> 00:11:34,319
his popularity, and his control over 
money, Moses had a lot of leverage,  

143
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:39,120
and he was not shy about using it.
Far from being worried about getting fired,  

144
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:42,879
Moses would constantly threaten to 
quit whenever he didn't get his way. 

145
00:11:42,879 --> 00:11:46,960
Under Mayor Wagner, one aide said 
that "it seemed like he was resigning  

146
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:53,360
about one thing or another about twice a day."
He threatened resignation to Mayor La Guardia so  

147
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:59,680
often and over such small things that La Guardia's 
lawyer got a stack of forms printed that read,  

148
00:11:59,679 --> 00:12:04,799
"I, Robert Moses, do hereby resign as 
so-and-so," to save Moses the trouble  

149
00:12:04,799 --> 00:12:09,199
of writing it out every single time.
But behind that joke was real fear. 

150
00:12:09,200 --> 00:12:14,400
If a mayor accepted that resignation, not only 
would he be forfeiting the completion of whatever  

151
00:12:14,399 --> 00:12:19,199
projects Moses was working on, along with the 
money that was apportioned for those projects,  

152
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:24,640
he'd also be picking a public fight with the 
most respected official in all of New York. 

153
00:12:24,639 --> 00:12:27,759
Maybe this is the most important 
thing: it's just sheer stubbornness. 

154
00:12:27,759 --> 00:12:31,360
Moses had spent years befriending 
the owners of the city's newspapers. 

155
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:34,480
He spent many weekends at the 
summer home of the Sulzbergers,  

156
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:39,600
the family that owns the New York Times.
The mayor knew that if Moses resigned,  

157
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:45,519
the next morning all the front pages would tell 
the same story, about how a man above politics  

158
00:12:45,519 --> 00:12:50,879
was driven out by a craven politician because 
he refused to compromise the city's interests. 

159
00:12:50,879 --> 00:12:55,439
And the power that Moses derived from his 
popularity with the masses was nothing compared  

160
00:12:55,440 --> 00:13:00,240
to the power that he derived from his connections 
with New York's powerful private interests. 

161
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:04,320
We are very used today to NIMBY, 
not in my backyard, where powerful  

162
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:10,160
but concentrated interest groups manage to 
stymie projects despite their raw popularity. 

163
00:13:10,159 --> 00:13:14,639
Moses built a concentrated faction 
for YIMBY, yes in my backyard. 

164
00:13:14,639 --> 00:13:19,439
He built a graft machine that ensured that 
the most powerful people in the state would  

165
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:24,400
all benefit when new construction happened.
The banks got to buy his bonds at a steep  

166
00:13:24,399 --> 00:13:28,879
discount without competitive bidding, the 
unions got more jobs, politically connected  

167
00:13:28,879 --> 00:13:34,320
lawyers got legal fees, and party bosses got 
paid to broker insurance policies on bridges  

168
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:39,680
that had no plausible risk of falling down.
Moses could then rely on these beneficiaries  

169
00:13:39,679 --> 00:13:43,919
to call up anyone who stood in the way of 
one of his projects, whether that was a  

170
00:13:43,919 --> 00:13:48,719
borough president or the governor himself.
Caro paints Moses as somebody who began  

171
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:52,399
as an idealist, rather than somebody 
who craved power from the very start. 

172
00:13:52,399 --> 00:13:57,919
In the 1910s, Moses was a brilliant young reformer 
who joined this new good government movement,  

173
00:13:57,919 --> 00:14:02,159
which was trying to replace Tammany 
Hall's patronage with honest and  

174
00:14:02,159 --> 00:14:05,439
competent administration.
He spent years fighting  

175
00:14:05,440 --> 00:14:11,040
this machine, but his reforms got killed.
The machine won, and Moses learned that ideals  

176
00:14:11,039 --> 00:14:17,199
are useless without the power to realize them.
Caro writes: "In the beginning, and for decades  

177
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:21,680
of his career, the power Robert Moses 
amassed was the servant of his dreams,  

178
00:14:21,679 --> 00:14:27,759
amassed for their sake, so that his gigantic, 
city-shaping visions could become reality. 

179
00:14:27,759 --> 00:14:31,600
But power is not an instrument that 
its possessor can use with impunity. 

180
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:37,840
It is a drug that creates in the user a need for 
larger and larger doses, and Moses was a user." 

181
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:45,120
Lyndon Johnson's story, on the 
other hand, is the opposite. 

182
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:47,360
Through most of his career, 
there's little evidence that  

183
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:51,919
he had a coherent political ideology.
He was a ruthless power seeker who  

184
00:14:51,919 --> 00:14:56,319
embraced whatever happened to be the 
most expedient thing to do at that time. 

185
00:14:56,320 --> 00:15:00,800
When Johnson first entered the US Senate, he 
realized that the most powerful men there were  

186
00:15:00,799 --> 00:15:04,879
the conservative Southern Dixiecrats, 
and the Johnson that they encountered  

187
00:15:04,879 --> 00:15:09,919
was one who molded himself to their form.
While writing Master of the Senate, volume 3  

188
00:15:09,919 --> 00:15:14,959
of his Johnson biographies, Caro interviewed 
former Georgia Senator Herman Talmadge. 

189
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:18,480
He asked Talmadge what impression 
he got from freshman senator Lyndon  

190
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:22,879
Johnson about the proper relationship 
between white people and Black people. 

191
00:15:22,879 --> 00:15:31,039
Now quoting Caro's recounting of this exchange: 
"'Master and servant.' So I," Caro, "asked, 'Well,  

192
00:15:31,039 --> 00:15:34,719
didn't Johnson have any sympathy for Blacks?
Any desire to improve their  

193
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:40,399
lot?' My notes say, long pause.
And then I wrote Talmadge's 2-word reply,  

194
00:15:40,399 --> 00:15:45,759
'None indicated.' Another pause, and 
then, 'He was with us in his heart. 

195
00:15:45,759 --> 00:15:52,240
I believed him. I believed him.'"
Of course, we know that once he became president,  

196
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:58,080
Johnson spearheaded the 1964 Civil Rights Act and 
the 1965 Voting Rights Act, the most important  

197
00:15:58,080 --> 00:16:03,520
civil rights laws since Reconstruction.
So Moses began as a naive idealist but,  

198
00:16:03,519 --> 00:16:07,519
in Caro's telling, became somebody 
seeking power for its own sake. 

199
00:16:07,519 --> 00:16:11,279
Johnson, on the other hand, was an 
opportunist from the very start,  

200
00:16:11,279 --> 00:16:16,720
and it was only once he had real power that 
he used it for ideals few knew that he had. 

201
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:20,800
Before he was president, Lyndon Johnson was vice 
president, and before he was vice president,  

202
00:16:20,799 --> 00:16:26,479
Lyndon Johnson was in the Senate, and Lyndon 
Johnson was the most powerful Senate majority  

203
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:30,720
leader, perhaps the most powerful legislator, 
period, in the history of this country. 

204
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:36,480
Here's Caro: "He was master of the Senate, 
master of an institution that had never  

205
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:41,200
before had a master, and that at the time 
almost half a century later, when this  

206
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:46,800
book is being written, has not had one since."
No leader before Johnson had wielded this kind  

207
00:16:46,799 --> 00:16:52,319
of power, because the Senate had deliberately 
been set up by the founders to not be led. 

208
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:57,680
When Johnson asked the Library of Congress for 
a list of the majority leader's formal powers,  

209
00:16:57,679 --> 00:17:01,519
he found that there was exactly 1: the 
right to be recognized first on the floor. 

210
00:17:01,519 --> 00:17:06,079
Before Johnson, the majority leader was 
less a leader and more a lightning rod. 

211
00:17:06,079 --> 00:17:10,159
He'd be blamed in the national press and 
in his home state for everything that the  

212
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:15,279
Senate failed to do, yet he had no actual 
power to do anything about those failures. 

213
00:17:15,279 --> 00:17:20,799
Being in this predicament was so politically 
toxic that Johnson's 2 predecessors as majority  

214
00:17:20,799 --> 00:17:25,359
leader had both lost their own Senate seats.
A big part of the reason that Johnson became  

215
00:17:25,359 --> 00:17:30,639
his party's leader at just 44 years old, in 
a Senate whose average age was around 60,  

216
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:35,200
was that hardly anybody else wanted 
this thankless and useless job. 

217
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:40,400
In their hands, the job would have remained 
useless, but they were not Lyndon Johnson. 

218
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:44,880
From this position, Johnson turned himself into 
the second most powerful person in America,  

219
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:48,880
right after the president, and he 
used 3 different tactics to do so. 

220
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:54,640
First, he gained leverage. He made sure that he 
had things to promise and things to threaten with. 

221
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:57,520
Johnson transformed the 
Senate's rules, institutions,  

222
00:17:57,519 --> 00:18:02,960
and norms so that he alone had the power to 
grant important favors to his colleagues. 

223
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:07,680
If a senator needed a bill passed, Johnson was the 
one who could get it scheduled on the floor and  

224
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:12,240
persuade the committee chairman to report it out.
If the bill needed help in the House,  

225
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:15,920
Johnson was the one who had a 
direct line to Speaker Sam Rayburn. 

226
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:19,680
And if the senator needed money for his next 
campaign so that he could make sure he remained  

227
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:24,880
a senator, Johnson was the one who could put in a 
good word with the oilmen from his native Texas. 

228
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:28,160
Johnson also put himself in a position 
to control committee assignments,  

229
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:32,800
which are the single biggest factor that 
shapes how a senator's whole career plays out. 

230
00:18:32,799 --> 00:18:36,480
For about a century, they had been 
handed out based on seniority. 

231
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:41,680
Practically overnight, Johnson changed the system 
so that many seats were no longer settled by  

232
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:47,279
seniority, but rather negotiated by the party 
steering committee, which Johnson controlled. 

233
00:18:47,279 --> 00:18:51,039
If you wanted a good seat, you 
had to do what Johnson wanted. 

234
00:18:51,039 --> 00:18:56,240
In 1957, Estes Kefauver voted against 
Johnson on an important motion. 

235
00:18:56,240 --> 00:19:01,440
That very same week, the highly coveted open seat 
on the Foreign Relations Committee, which Kefauver  

236
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:08,799
was expecting to get, was instead handed over to 
a far more junior senator named John F. Kennedy. 

237
00:19:08,799 --> 00:19:15,359
The real political genius of Lyndon Johnson was 
identifying and then gaining control over every  

238
00:19:15,359 --> 00:19:20,399
possible source of leverage over his colleagues.
Whatever a senator needed to survive,  

239
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:26,240
whether that was money, committee seats, or the 
passage of bills that mattered to his home state,  

240
00:19:26,240 --> 00:19:30,559
Johnson could give and Johnson could withhold.
The second important tactic  

241
00:19:30,559 --> 00:19:33,359
was setting the agenda.
What is said by a bill that  

242
00:19:33,359 --> 00:19:39,359
passes depends on which version actually comes 
up for a vote and with what final modifications. 

243
00:19:39,359 --> 00:19:42,639
Whoever gets to set this 
agenda controls the outcome. 

244
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:46,560
So you won't be surprised by who 
put himself in this position. 

245
00:19:46,559 --> 00:19:51,440
Johnson's main tool for controlling the 
agenda was the unanimous consent agreement. 

246
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:55,840
By default, Senate debate is unlimited.
A senator can talk about a bill for as long  

247
00:19:55,839 --> 00:20:00,959
as he likes, and as he does so, he can also offer 
amendments that have nothing to do with that bill,  

248
00:20:00,960 --> 00:20:05,840
each of which can stall the bill even further.
A unanimous consent agreement sets those  

249
00:20:05,839 --> 00:20:09,519
freedoms aside for a single bill.
It fixes the allotted time for debate  

250
00:20:09,519 --> 00:20:14,960
and constrains the opportunity to amend it.
Earlier leaders had rarely used such agreements,  

251
00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:18,400
and even when they had, they'd only used 
them after weeks of debate on a bill that  

252
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:24,400
had already been exhausted in its deliberations.
Johnson made their use the norm in the Senate. 

253
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:29,360
He wouldn't even allow a bill to reach the 
floor until such an agreement was in place. 

254
00:20:29,359 --> 00:20:32,879
The reason was that Johnson wanted to 
control which bills reached the floor  

255
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:37,600
and to keep the floor from changing anybody's 
mind, because he wanted every vote settled  

256
00:20:37,599 --> 00:20:42,159
in private beforehand through the wheeling 
and dealing where his leverage worked best. 

257
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:46,960
The last thing he wanted was for a long public 
debate about the merits of some bill to change a  

258
00:20:46,960 --> 00:20:51,840
senator's mind at the very last minute, or for a 
surprise amendment to unravel a deal that he had  

259
00:20:51,839 --> 00:20:55,839
spent weeks painstakingly brokering.
As Senator Paul Douglas put it,  

260
00:20:55,839 --> 00:21:00,399
under Johnson, all the action takes 
place offstage before the play begins. 

261
00:21:00,400 --> 00:21:05,360
The third tactic that Johnson employed was 
brokering all the deals to his advantage. 

262
00:21:05,359 --> 00:21:09,599
Johnson made sure that as much of the Senate's 
information as possible ran through him. 

263
00:21:09,599 --> 00:21:12,959
He had every committee assign a staffer 
to tell him what was being drafted. 

264
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:17,920
His aides tracked what every senator was up to.
And he spent his own days in the cloakroom and  

265
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:21,039
in the hallways cajoling and 
listening to his colleagues. 

266
00:21:21,039 --> 00:21:26,480
So he knew exactly what each senator really wanted 
and what he would and wouldn't give up to get it. 

267
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:31,120
In any session, the Senate faces hundreds 
of possible bills and amendments. 

268
00:21:31,119 --> 00:21:35,679
A senator's vote on one bill can be traded 
for another senator's vote on a different one. 

269
00:21:35,680 --> 00:21:40,000
And any single bill can be amended a 
dozen different ways to win somebody over. 

270
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:44,079
So there's almost an endless combination 
of possible deals to be made. 

271
00:21:44,079 --> 00:21:48,960
Johnson made sure that he was the one 
instigating and brokering most of these deals. 

272
00:21:48,960 --> 00:21:54,079
As a result, he would be making sure that 
any deal left Johnson coming out ahead. 

273
00:21:54,079 --> 00:21:58,720
A good example is how he got the Civil 
Rights Act of 1957 passed through the Senate. 

274
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:01,600
Johnson was desperate to make sure 
that at least some civil rights  

275
00:22:01,599 --> 00:22:05,919
bill passed that year, even if it was weak.
That's because he wanted to become president,  

276
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:11,120
but no Southerner with his record of consistently 
voting against civil rights could hope to win the  

277
00:22:11,119 --> 00:22:16,799
Democratic presidential nomination in 1960.
The problem was there seemed to be no bill  

278
00:22:16,799 --> 00:22:20,240
strong enough to win the Northern 
liberals, yet weak enough that  

279
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:24,720
the Southerners wouldn't filibuster it.
The sticking point was who would preside over  

280
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:29,759
the court cases where Southern electoral officials 
were accused of suppressing Black voters. 

281
00:22:29,759 --> 00:22:34,879
Southern senators insisted on jury trials, 
but Northern senators were livid because,  

282
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:40,080
in their view, this would gut the whole bill.
The entire point of the law was to combat the  

283
00:22:40,079 --> 00:22:44,799
exclusion of Black citizens from the voting rolls.
But Southern states required jurors to be  

284
00:22:44,799 --> 00:22:49,839
registered voters, and an all-white Southern 
jury would never convict a white registrar  

285
00:22:49,839 --> 00:22:53,279
for turning away Black voters.
So any version that one side  

286
00:22:53,279 --> 00:22:59,200
could accept, the other would reject.
A less creative legislator would have backed out. 

287
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:04,960
But over the course of 7 months, Johnson 
built a masterful deal that brought together  

288
00:23:04,960 --> 00:23:10,319
wholly unrelated factions and whose byproduct 
included the passage of this civil rights law. 

289
00:23:10,319 --> 00:23:13,519
Western senators got a vote on a 
federal dam that they had wanted  

290
00:23:13,519 --> 00:23:17,519
for years between Idaho and Oregon.
Midwestern Republicans who had powerful  

291
00:23:17,519 --> 00:23:22,400
railroad unions in their states got the right 
to a jury trial extended to striking workers. 

292
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:26,960
And moderates got a clause guaranteeing that 
Black citizens could serve on federal juries,  

293
00:23:26,960 --> 00:23:30,480
so that they could tell voters that 
they had created a new civil right. 

294
00:23:30,480 --> 00:23:35,360
Meanwhile, the jury trial guarantee for 
Southern election officials stayed in,  

295
00:23:35,359 --> 00:23:39,439
which kept the bill weak enough that 
Southerners agreed not to filibuster it. 

296
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:44,720
A dam in Idaho and protections for union 
workers and federal jury rules had nothing  

297
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:50,000
to do with voting rights in the South, at least 
not to anyone who lacked the inside knowledge  

298
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:58,799
and political creativity of Lyndon Johnson.
Caro's books are the best-written accounts  

299
00:23:58,799 --> 00:24:03,279
of how political power actually works.
But they are written so compellingly, with  

300
00:24:03,279 --> 00:24:09,119
characters more vivid and stories more gripping 
than any novel, that it's very easy to get so  

301
00:24:09,119 --> 00:24:15,359
engrossed that you just keep turning the pages 
and never stop and ask yourself, what are the  

302
00:24:15,359 --> 00:24:20,319
lessons these stories are teaching about power?
I wanted to make this video essay so that I could  

303
00:24:20,319 --> 00:24:24,399
think through what these lessons actually 
are, but also so that you would have the  

304
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:29,280
context necessary to watch my interview with 
Caro and get as much out of it as I did. 

305
00:24:29,279 --> 00:24:32,639
You'll be able to watch the interview tomorrow.
So by the time you're listening to this,  

306
00:24:32,640 --> 00:24:38,720
it might already be out.
I hope you enjoy it.
